+The main NMS study uses one laptop CPU, float32 inference on exactly one pinned core. On this configuration the forward pass dominates (Table~\ref{tab:aa}), so the NMS share is small and, by the Amdahl model, end-to-end increases are compressed: the design is biased towards a ``no effect'' conclusion. Published latency-attack claims come from GPUs or edge devices with accelerators; the main study does not reproduce those deployment conditions, so it does not refute those claims but provides a tool to check them. A separately preregistered segmentation extension on a rented CPU/GPU host is reported in Appendix~\ref{app:segmentation}; its cohorts and endpoints are kept separate from the main study. We attempted measurements on an Arm smartphone but could not verify core pinning under load, so no results are reported for that device. Earlier measurements on a physical Raspberry Pi fail their preregistered swap or pilot precision gates and are retained as rejected evidence (Appendices~\ref{app:seg-pi3b-diagnostic} and~\ref{app:seg-pi3b-swapguard}). A later, separately registered full-cohort segmentation replay qualifies total-time estimates on the declared 600~MHz Pi 3B condition, while its component session fails NMS precision (Appendix~\ref{app:seg-pi3b-full20}); it does not establish a service deadline failure or denial of service.
